itors users accounts  and can automatically repo plane tickets  when prices drop request refunds when  banks charge illegal overdraft fees and  help users reclaim security deposits  from shady landlords the flagship  feature is if you have a problem with  anyone via a corporation or your  neighbor you can sue them by pressing a  button what happens when people actually  get to court to contest these things so  at that point we actually give you a  script which you can read yourself in  the future I think robots will actually  be allowed in court but until that day  comes people actually have to shop  Sal's but they can almost be conduit for  the robot what we give you is very  legally sound and you can actually play  a game of almost learning what you have  to say with the robot lawyer in the app  router says the legal industry is so  ripe for disruption through software  because most services involve nothing  more than standardized processes in  boilerplate language I'm very lucky  although I hate lawyers we actually have  a few good ones who are helping us 50  states with a bunch of different  jurisdictions within them you cover that  entire gamut of different legal  structures yeah at the end of the day  lawyers like to make it seem like the  Laura's rocket science but in reality  the Laura's society's operating system  and the rules should just be able to be  coded without some fancier AI we have  got a bit of it on top but really it's  just a rules-based system given that why  our legal services so expensive right  now if you're a lawyer there's no  incentive to innovate instead you can  just be making millions of dollars a  year by charging clients huge amounts of  money and so because lawyers control the  legal system no one else really  understands it they've kept it that way  and I think it will take people outside  of the legal profession like software to  force them to change and what's your  response to people that say you know  you're building into things like  immigration yes not just a standardized  parking ticket you only have a few  lawyers helping you yeah you can't  possibly be providing the level of  service that a trained legal  professional would I think we really  have a long way to go but on the other  hand if it really is that different for  every person doesn't that mean it's  unfair do you also hope to sort of  incentivize the broader legal system to  become more simple and fair in this way  yes and I think the only way to do that  is by am doing bottom-up approach I like  to think of it as ddossing the legal  system to make it better one of the most  exciting things we have coming out is  something that can get you a DMV  appointment in a few days rather than  months by phoning up the DMV hundreds of  times a day and talking to their  automated scheduling system to try and  look for a cancellation for you so this  sounds great on my end it sounds like a  nightmare from a bureaucrats end or a  regulator or somebody who works the DMV  yeah I have you gotten any pushback on  any of these initiatives we think hard  about doing in an ethical way so we're  only talking to their robots not real  human beings holding up the phone lines  and in fact a lot of governments welcome  do not  when do not pay parking tickets came to  Los Angeles and they said we love it  because people write such gibberish and  there appeals that at least do not pay  Appeals are standardized and well  written so I think that in the long run  and the government's will build their  own systems for dealing with all of this  traffic which will lead to a much fairer  outcome for everyone do not pay doesn't  charge for any of its legal help this  way the company can serve clients who  can't afford to hire lawyers such as  refugees seeking asylum and homeless  people applying for public housing in  the UK the company is also working on a  service that helps navigate the u.s.  visa process all of these processes are  so bureaucratic that if you have no  resources at all it really is impossible  to get the help you need so do not pay  as a completely free public service so  how do you sustain yourself how are you  making money although we have to make  money to sustain ourselves we're not  going to be doing it for charging for  our legal services maybe one day we will  help so many people for example fire  airlines that we will learn how to do an  airline ourselves maybe do not pay  airlines that's a big example but we aim  to replace the people with fighting  that's how we'll make money but I'm not  focused on that at the moment because  everything is fully automated  we're very cost efficient and there are  other similar services or at least ones  that allow you to automatically contest  parking tickets yeah they make money by  taking the slice of the revenue that  they return to people why didn't you  adopt that sort of model 50% of  injustice is still injustice and by  profiting off the problems these  companies actually have an incentive to  perpetuate them which is why they  haven't been solved I think the biggest  example are all these tax filing  services which are now lobbying the  government to keep tax filing  complicated the government has tried to  have a much simpler tax filing process  but the companies publicly listed  companies who they made money off that  sort of thing are saying no we want it  to stay complicated  so do not pay doesn't have any of the  conflict of interest but Browder does  advocate for more consumer protection  laws do not pay never relies on  corporate policies to get customers  money back on things like airline  tickets using only regulations buried in  fine print the way we do it is through  these obscure Department of  Transportation rules that allow the most  non-refundable ticket to become  refundable and that's really important  because laws are hard to change  of course all these corporations have  lobbyists but it's much harder for them  to repeal airline protections than it is  for them to just change a customer  service policy overnight do you feel  that these consumer protections are more  necessary in the airline industry or  because like you were saying earlier a  lot of what you do is help people  navigate complexity the complexity  usually ends up hurting the less  well-off because they're not able to  fund actually going through it don't  have the time so in other areas where  like tax preparation it's better if it's  more simplistic why is the deregulatory  push in the airline space a problem I'm  from the UK and one of the biggest  differences I've noticed between the US  and the UK is that in the u.s. is the  land of the corporation in the UK there  are so many rights that everyone has for  example if the airline changes your  flight time you have a right to cancel I  think that's a very reasonable right for  people to have and do not pay helps  people exercise those rights so it's not  about complexity it's just about giving  people what they deserve and what's just  and fair so in the long run yeah how do  you see do not pay sort of expanding  what space you'd occupying and what do  you think the future is of legal  services once yeah everything can be  automated including a robot lawyer  coming with you to a court room in  Silicon Valley companies like Airbnb and  Twitter have succeeded by adding words  the dictionary you can tweet something  or snap something I think do not pay  will have succeeded if the web lawyer is  completely removed from the dictionary  for average people there's a two hundred  billion dollar legal industry at the  moment and do not pay well hopefully one  day make it free for everyone  [Music]

